592	    /// Spec §5.1: the diagnostic lands in Temporal history and must never carry
593	    /// payload bytes.
594	    ///
595	    /// The payload is a bare JSON **string** decoded against the envelope arm,
596	    /// which expects a struct. That combination is the one that actually leaks:
597	    /// serde_json renders it as `invalid type: string "<content>", expected
598	    /// struct ...`, embedding the value verbatim. A map-vs-string mismatch would
599	    /// NOT prove anything here — `Unexpected::Map` never echoes nested content,
600	    /// so such a test passes whether or not `decode_arg` discards its source
601	    /// error.
602	    ///
603	    /// This is also the only test that exercises the **envelope** arm's
604	    /// (arity 1) error path — every `*_content_failure_is_encoding_error` test
605	    /// feeds a legacy `MultiArgs{N}` shape instead. So it additionally asserts
606	    /// the error variant is `EncodingError`, not just sentinel-absent: if the
607	    /// envelope arm ever regressed to returning `WrongEncoding`, the composite
608	    /// converter would silently fall through to the `serde_json` arm (which
609	    /// also yields `WrongEncoding`), and production would lose this
610	    /// diagnostic — a bare "Wrong encoding" in Temporal history — while every
611	    /// other test here kept passing.
612	    #[test]
